    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d66">

                <pb facs="0778" n="628" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg628"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 3691/384.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Brown to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">La Union,
                                Salvador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-04-11"><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Brown reports that peace is assured and that the Government of Salvador
                    accepts considerate propositions made through him by the President of Nicaragua
                    for a peace conference in the port of Amapala between the two presidents in
                    person immediately upon <pb facs="0779" n="629" xml:id="pg629"/> the surrender
                    of that port, which he expects will be effected within twenty-four hours. He
                    also reports that the use of American war vessels is desired and needed for this
                    purpose, and he would request instructions to war vessels in order to be able to
                    offer all necessary courtesies and facilities for an immediate conference. Mr.
                    Brown further reports that the President of Nicaragua expressed grateful
                    appreciation of the friendly attitude of the Government of the United States in
                    behalf of peace in Central America.</p>
            </div>
